00:02:19.085 --> 00:02:21.725
Do you sell, pay the inclusion rate, and write a check?

00:02:22.265 --> 00:02:26.125
Or do you instruct the brokerage to move the shares into the Charities account and claim

00:02:26.125 --> 00:02:28.125
the donation receipt at the higher value?

00:02:28.845 --> 00:02:33.105
I'd transfer the shares because the inclusion rate never fires and the receipt is still

00:02:33.105 --> 00:02:34.245
the full market value.

00:02:34.745 --> 00:02:35.805
The step is mechanical.

00:02:36.405 --> 00:02:40.345
You tell the brokerage to send the shares, not the cash, then you file the receipt.

00:02:40.745 --> 00:02:42.825
It only works in a non-registered account.

00:02:42.825 --> 00:02:48.245
It fails if the position is under water, and it fails if the charity cannot take an electronic transfer.

00:02:48.805 --> 00:02:53.445
Plan the size against the annual donation limit, and against the capital gains inclusion rate

00:02:53.445 --> 00:02:57.825
you would have paid on a sale. And you can line the gift up with tax-free savings account,

00:02:58.085 --> 00:03:04.545
or TFSA, contribution room math so the after-tax hit is spread across accounts instead of dumped

00:03:04.545 --> 00:03:09.125
into one year. Confirm the charity can receive the transfer before you start the move.

00:03:09.125 --> 00:03:14.025
Done right, the charity keeps the whole market value, and you never book the tax that a cash

00:03:14.025 --> 00:03:15.025
